From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 18:20:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): a pane in a background window is not focused
 (KD-04)

A ghostty pane in a window that never came to the front blinked a
focused-looking cursor for as long as it lived, and paid a full grid
rebuild and a full-surface present every 600 ms for each blink -
measured at an unbroken 1.7/sec through the whole life of such a window.

KD-04 blamed a missing LostFocus. That was wrong, and its own numbers
said so: `notify` never moved, and a focus change queues a render, so
the surface was never told anything about focus in either direction.
Tracing every focus call shows LostFocus does arrive when another window
takes the foreground, and the blink stops there correctly.

What actually happens is at the other end. XAML's routed focus is
app-internal: a control in a window that has never been activated still
gets GotFocus, because logical focus lives inside the app's own tree.
Nothing contradicts it afterwards, because no deactivation can follow an
activation that never happened. So the pane believes it is focused
forever - blinking cursor, focus reported to the application, renderer
woken twice a second.

WM_ACTIVATE does not answer the question either. WT's activation signal
reports the active window of the thread's input queue, not the
foreground: TerminalPage::WindowActivated(1) arrives while
GetForegroundWindow() is another application's window. A first version
of this gated on that flag and changed nothing at all.

So TermControl::_pushCoreFocus sends the core routed focus AND the
window really being in front (GetForegroundWindow() == OwningHwnd()),
and only on a change, since GotFocus/LostFocus enable and disable the
UIA engine and send the DECSET 1004 focus reports. Activation stays as a
trigger to re-evaluate - fanned out from TerminalPage::WindowActivated
the way WindowVisibilityChanged already is, with the initial state set
in _SetupControl - never as the answer. XAML's GotFocus is a second
trigger and it arrives after the foreground has changed, so a genuine
activation is evaluated at least once with the foreground correct.

GhosttyControlCore::_createSurface also pushes the initial state: a
libghostty surface is born believing it is focused, and libghostty's own
comment says "it is up to the apprt to set the correct value". We are
the apprt.

Measured on the running app, all four cases: in front -> focused and
blinking at 1.7/sec; foreground moves away -> LostFocus, blink stops;
foreground returns -> blinking resumes; never in front -> GotFocus with
inFront=0, never focused, never blinks. scripts\probe-idle-focus.ps1
reports 7 blink reports before backgrounding against 1 in the following
15 seconds.

The gate is in TermControl, which both engines share, so cascadia sees
it too: a cascadia pane in a never-activated window now also comes up
unfocused, which is what its renderer's initial cursor-visibility
inhibitor already assumed. Suites unchanged: terminalCore 54/54,
unitSettingsModel 159/159, unitControl 63/63, localTerminalApp 76/76.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/cascadia/TerminalApp/TerminalPage.cpp     | 24 +++++++
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 23 ++++++
 src/cascadia/TerminalControl/TermControl.cpp  | 70 ++++++++++++++++---
 src/cascadia/TerminalControl/TermControl.h    |  8 +++
 src/cascadia/TerminalControl/TermControl.idl  |  1 +
 5 files changed, 118 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalApp/TerminalPage.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalApp/TerminalPage.cpp
index 6ac715084..658b93520 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalApp/TerminalPage.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalApp/TerminalPage.cpp
@@ -3739,6 +3739,13 @@ namespace winrt::TerminalApp::implementation
             term.WindowVisibilityChanged(_visible);
         }
 
+        // A control is born assuming its window is not active, because that is
+        // the case nothing else would ever correct: a window that is never
+        // activated raises no WM_ACTIVATE, so the fan-out in WindowActivated
+        // never runs. A control created into an already-active window has to be
+        // told here instead - including a tab torn off into another window.
+        term.WindowActivated(_activated);
+
         // Even in the case of re-attaching content from another window, this
         // will correctly update the control's owning HWND
         if (_hostingHwnd.has_value())
@@ -5420,6 +5427,23 @@ namespace winrt::TerminalApp::implementation
         _activated = activated;
         _updateThemeColors();
 
+        // Every control needs this, not just the focused one: XAML's routed
+        // focus stays inside the app, so a control keeps logical focus while its
+        // window sits in the background, and the terminal underneath it goes on
+        // believing it is focused. Only the two together mean focused. KD-04.
+        for (const auto& tab : _tabs)
+        {
+            if (const auto tabImpl{ _GetTabImpl(tab) })
+            {
+                tabImpl->GetRootPane()->WalkTree([activated](const auto& pane) {
+                    if (const auto& control{ pane->GetTerminalControl() })
+                    {
+                        control.WindowActivated(activated);
+                    }
+                });
+            }
+        }
+
         _adjustProcessPriorityThrottled->Run();
 
         if (const auto& tab{ _GetFocusedTabImpl() })
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 063a7c13d..30baf9038 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -120,6 +120,23 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
         _pushSettings();
 
+        // Tell the surface whether it is focused. A surface is born believing it
+        // is - libghostty's own comment says "this defaults to true and it is up
+        // to the apprt to set the correct value" (renderer/Thread.zig) - and we
+        // are the apprt, so leaving it unsaid means a pane that comes up in a
+        // background window blinks a focused-looking cursor and pays a rebuild
+        // and a full-surface present every 600 ms for it. The gate that decides
+        // this is TermControl::_pushCoreFocus; this is the starting position it
+        // needs, and it is the same one cascadia's renderer takes (the Host
+        // cursor-visibility inhibitor is set until something says focused). KD-04.
+        //
+        // _focused may already be true: if XAML focused the control before the
+        // panel had a size to initialize from, GotFocus arrived while _surface
+        // was still null and dropped its call. Pushing the tracked value rather
+        // than a literal false covers that order as well as the ordinary one.
+        _trace(L"initial focus = %d", _focused ? 1 : 0);
+        ghostty_surface_set_focus(_surface, _focused);
+
         ghostty_surface_set_content_scale(_surface, scale, scale);
         SizeChanged(width, height);
     }
@@ -892,8 +909,13 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         ghostty_surface_text(_surface, utf8.data(), utf8.size());
     }
 
+    // Focus is traced because it is not observable any other way and it decides
+    // whether an idle pane costs a rebuild and a present every 600 ms. These
+    // are XAML's *routed* focus events on the control, which is not the same
+    // thing as the window being active - see _createSurface and KD-04.
     void GhosttyControlCore::GotFocus()
     {
+        _trace(L"GotFocus (surface=%p)", _surface);
         _focused = true;
         if (_surface)
         {
@@ -903,6 +925,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
     void GhosttyControlCore::LostFocus()
     {
+        _trace(L"LostFocus (surface=%p)", _surface);
         _focused = false;
         if (_surface)
         {
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/TermControl.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/TermControl.cpp
index 827998147..2c313b080 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/TermControl.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/TermControl.cpp
@@ -2495,10 +2495,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // That way, new selections are notified to automation clients.
         // The _uiaEngine lives in _interactivity, so call into there to enable it.
 
-        if (_interactivity)
-        {
-            _interactivity.GotFocus();
-        }
+        _pushCoreFocus();
 
         // If the searchbox is focused, we don't want TSFInputControl to think
         // it has focus so it doesn't intercept IME input. We also don't want the
@@ -2541,10 +2538,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
         // This will disable the accessibility notifications, because the
         // UiaEngine lives in ControlInteractivity
-        if (_interactivity)
-        {
-            _interactivity.LostFocus();
-        }
+        _pushCoreFocus();
 
         // Check if there is an unfocused config we should set the appearance to
         // upon losing focus
@@ -3077,6 +3071,66 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         _core.WindowVisibilityChanged(showOrHide);
     }
 
+    // Method Description:
+    // - Tells the control whether its *window* is the active one. This is a
+    //   different question from whether the control has focus: XAML's routed
+    //   GotFocus/LostFocus are app-internal, and a control in a window that has
+    //   never been activated still gets GotFocus, keeping logical focus inside
+    //   its own app. A terminal is only really focused when both are true.
+    // - Without this, a pane in a window that is never brought to the front
+    //   believes it is focused for as long as it lives: it shows a blinking,
+    //   focused-looking cursor, tells the application it has focus, and - on the
+    //   ghostty engine, whose renderer wakes for each blink - rebuilds and
+    //   presents the whole surface every 600 ms. Deactivation was never the
+    //   broken half; a control that had focus does get LostFocus when another
+    //   window takes the foreground, measured. KD-04.
+    // Arguments:
+    // - activated: whether the hosting window is now the active window.
+    void TermControl::WindowActivated(const bool activated)
+    {
+        _windowActivated = activated;
+        _pushCoreFocus();
+    }
+
+    // Method Description:
+    // - Send the core the focus state it should act on - routed focus AND the
+    //   window really being in front - and only when it changes. The core's
+    //   GotFocus/LostFocus are not idempotent for what is downstream of them:
+    //   they enable and disable the UIA engine and send the focus-reporting
+    //   escapes (DECSET 1004) to the application.
+    // - The window half is read from the *foreground window*, not from the
+    //   activation flag, because WM_ACTIVATE does not answer this question. It
+    //   reports the active window of the thread's input queue, so a window
+    //   created while another application is in front is told it is active:
+    //   measured, `TerminalPage::WindowActivated(1)` while GetForegroundWindow()
+    //   was another app's window. Activation is still worth having as a trigger,
+    //   which is what WindowActivated is for - and XAML's GotFocus is a second
+    //   one, arriving after the foreground has changed, so a genuine activation
+    //   is evaluated at least once with the foreground already correct.
+    void TermControl::_pushCoreFocus()
+    {
+        const auto owning = reinterpret_cast<HWND>(OwningHwnd());
+        // No owning window (content not yet attached to one) leaves nothing to
+        // compare against; trust the activation flag rather than guess.
+        const auto windowInFront = owning ? (::GetForegroundWindow() == owning) : _windowActivated;
+
+        const auto focused = _focused && windowInFront;
+        if (focused == _coreFocused || !_interactivity)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+        _coreFocused = focused;
+
+        if (focused)
+        {
+            _interactivity.GotFocus();
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            _interactivity.LostFocus();
+        }
+    }
+
     // Method Description:
     // - Get the modifier keys that are currently pressed. This can be used to
     //   find out which modifiers (ctrl, alt, shift) are pressed in events that
